LN(1)                       User Commands

NAME
    ln - make hard links and symbolic links

SYNOPSIS
    ln [-sf] target [name]
    ln [-sf] target... directory

DESCRIPTION
    ln creates name as a hard link to the file target, or as a symbolic
    link holding the text target when -s is given. Without a name the link
    is created in the working directory under the last component of
    target. When the last operand is a directory, or a symbolic link to
    one, each target receives a link in it under its last component. -f
    removes an existing name that is not a directory first.

    A symbolic link stores target as given and does not check that it
    exists. A relative target is resolved from the directory that holds
    the link, not from the working directory, and a target may hold at
    most 255 bytes. A hard link needs both names on one filesystem; FAT
    has neither kind of link, devfs has no symbolic links, and ln then
    prints the error the kernel returns and exits with status 1.

EXAMPLES
    ln -s /etc/motd motd
    ln -s /usr/share/man/man1 /home/man1
    ln -sf new.conf current.conf

SEE ALSO
    readlink(1), ls(1), rm(1), cp(1)
